+# Hacker News Research Brief
+
+_Generated on 2026-07-14 11:10:11_
+
+## Per-article insights
+
+### 1. Measuring Input Latency on Linux: X11 vs. Wayland, VRR, and DXVK
+
+- **Source ID:** `48909424`
+- **URL:** <https://marco-nett.de/blog/measuring-input-latency-on-linux-x11-vs-wayland-vrr-dxvk/>
+- **Sentiment:** neutral
+
+The article describes a custom hardware-based method to measure end-to-end input latency on Linux gaming setups and applies it to compare X11, Wayland, VRR, and dxvk-low-latency. In the reported tests, X11 is slightly faster than Wayland, VRR shows the largest consistent latency improvement, and dxvk-low-latency appears to help modestly in capped cases while offering more value in uncapped scenarios.
+
+**Key points**
+
+- The author built a device to measure end-to-end latency by timing the interval between a simulated click and a detected screen brightness change.
+- X11 was faster than Wayland in every tested scenario, but only by 0.14 to 0.22 ms.
+- VRR had the biggest measured impact, improving latency by 0.26 to 0.45 ms and narrowing the latency distribution.
+- In capped test cases, dxvk-low-latency was about as beneficial as the X11 versus Wayland difference, and the author says its main strength appears in uncapped cases.
